## Context

Tracker: 0001. 0003 is closed, and its verification line

    - [ ] Peak RSS stays within a few hundred MB, against 6,48 GB today

has now been measured on the real library and is **not met**. Filed as its own
ticket rather than reopening 0003, whose other criteria did pass.

Measured 2026-08-21 on the 7 540-item library, uncapped, full text on, stock
Node with no heap flag. Two runs, one under concurrent load and one clean,
byte-identical in every content figure:

| | run 1 (contended) | run 2 (clean) |
|---|---|---|
| build wall clock | 374 s | 339 s |
| **peak RSS** (`VmHWM`) | 1 892 MiB | **2 085 MiB** |
| RSS at end of build | 382 MiB | 327 MiB |
| RSS serving, after 8 queries | — | **135 MiB** |

Peak RSS is per-process, so contention did not cause it: it reproduced.

## What this does and does not damage

**The chantier's headline survives intact.** The claim that matters is
resident memory *at rest* -- 5 370 MB for the JS index against **121-135 MiB**
here, and an index that reloads on stock Node instead of OOMing. That is
measured and holds.

**What fails is the build-time claim**, and only by comparison with the
ticket's own wording. 2 085 MiB is still 2,6x below the 5 370 MB resident
figure and 3,2x below the 6,48 GB build peak 0003 cites. It is not a
regression against zoteus; it is a promise this chantier should not have made
in that form. The honest headline is **"~2 GB peak during build, ~130 MB at
rest"**, and every place that currently says "a few hundred MB" needs to say
that instead.

## The hypothesis, which is not yet a finding

The RSS curve climbs monotonically to its peak at t≈253 s, collapses to
~220 MiB, then sawtooths 240-400 MiB to the end. That shape is consistent with
V8 old-space growing until a major GC rather than with a leak.

A candidate cause: `buildIncremental`'s page loop resolves full text for a
whole page before chunking any of it, so 100 uncapped attachment texts are
live simultaneously. At this library's sizes that is plausibly a gigabyte.

**This has not been isolated.** It is an observation plus a guess, and the
guess is cheap to test by varying `fulltextConcurrency` and page size and
re-reading `VmHWM`. Do that before changing anything -- the failure mode here
is fixing the wrong thing because the shape looked familiar.

## Actions

0. **Cheapest first, added 2026-08-22:** one item (`DH8EXSVA`, the New Palgrave
   Dictionary) contributes 44,9 MB of text and 9% of all passages -- see
   ticket 0013. Rebuild with it excluded and re-read `VmHWM` before doing
   anything else. A single 44,9 MB string live in the page loop is a far more
   specific suspect than "100 uncapped texts", and this experiment is minutes
   rather than a sweep.
1. If that does not account for the peak: vary `fulltextConcurrency`
   (currently defaults to 4) and the page size, measure `VmHWM` for each, and
   establish whether the page loop is in fact the cause.
2. If it is: stream the page rather than holding it -- chunk and insert each
   item's text as it arrives, so a page's worth is never simultaneously live.
   The rows already go to disk per item; it is the *source text* that
   accumulates.
3. Re-measure and record.
4. Correct the wording everywhere it appears: `STATE.md`, ticket 0001's
   claims, and whatever goes into the oscardvs/zoteus#10 report. **The #10
   number must be the measured one**, not the one this chantier hoped for.

## Test

Read `VmHWM` from `/proc/<pid>/status` after a build, not a sampled `VmRSS`:
the kernel's high-water mark cannot miss a peak between samples. `bench/run_build.py`
does this already and is the reference. Assert nothing in the unit suite --
this is a measurement, and a memory assertion in CI would be machine-dependent.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Cause of the ~2 GB peak isolated, or explicitly recorded as not isolated
      after the exclude-0013 test and then the concurrency/page-size sweep
- [ ] If reducible, reduced and re-measured; if not, the number documented as
      inherent
- [ ] "A few hundred MB" corrected to the measured figure in STATE, 0001, and
      the #10 report
